--TEST--
Every PHP spelling the types chapter drops is refused, swept in one program so the count of refusals is the assertion rather than any one row — `rule:types/conversion`, `rule:classes/no-free-functions-or-constants`, `rule:expressions/bracket-destructuring`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: lang:types/what-does-not-exist
// The chapter's last table is a list of spellings a program arriving from PHP
// still contains, each with the one thing to write instead. This case offers
// every one of them to the compiler at once, so a row that quietly started
// compiling loses an error while every other line still looks right. The
// replacements themselves are pinned by the case beside this one.
class Holder {
    public int $n = 1;

    public static function twice(int $n): int {
        return $n * 2;
    }
}

mixed $m = 1;
string $s = "abc";
array<int> $pair = [1, 2];
Holder $h = new Holder();

int $cast = (int)$m;
int $converted = intval($m);
settype($m, "string");
echo gettype($m), "\n";
echo is_int($m), "\n";
resource $handle = 1;
callable $named = "Holder::twice";
callable $bound = [$h, "twice"];
echo $s[0], "\n";
echo "3" * 2, "\n";
echo "3" == 3 ? "y" : "n", "\n";
echo "a" < "b" ? "y" : "n", "\n";
list($x, $y) = $pair;
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
